{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request ad5955ea, CE-2: explicit recovery, as you propose.\nNo automatic recovery when a request meets `full`.\n\nReasons. `settle` is already the call that an agent makes after a\nlost reply, so the proof belongs there. An automatic sweep needs a\ntrigger, a bound, a continuation and a rule for targets that it\ncannot read. Each of those is new behavior that the agent cannot\nsee. One proof for one named request is visible and bounded.\n\nYour three answers stand, with these points.\n\n1. `settle` of a request whose latest signing is dead runs the\n   proof of your item 2 and then calls `absent`. A repeated CLI\n   install of the same request does the same. No other call ends a\n   record.\n2. Each failed proof leaves the record as it is and says why: no\n   session, target not readable, journal filtered, refused,\n   archived or incomplete, or a capability step.\n3. The `full` answer tells the agent what to do. Its `next` text\n   says to settle an earlier request. Add the key of the oldest\n   unresolved record with a dead signing if the store gives it\n   with no scan under the count lock. If it does not, say so in the\n   note and keep the plain text.\n4. The answer shapes of NF-2 stay. Your wording for `unsent`\n   stands, for MCP and for the CLI.\n5. The witness is yours: 256 real requests, each reply lost, the\n   clock past expiry, `not-found` alone leaves admission full, one\n   recovery, then request 257 signs.\n6. State in the note what one recovery reads: the number of log\n   entries and reads for the witness journal. Change nothing for\n   cost before I see that number.\n\nOrder stays low. CE-2 follows C3 on main, in one head with one\ngate.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ad5955ea9c2766071523bd4c50eaed507d573d8b stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:003802bea882e6c5c61885187bad460807f9b07f, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f